00:43:20.565 --> 00:43:25.034
There's such a thing
as doing well by doing good.

491
00:43:25.036 --> 00:43:27.070
And you haven't answered
my question.

492
00:43:27.072 --> 00:43:28.504
Bobby Fischer...

493
00:43:30.274 --> 00:43:34.143
Bobby Fischer is the second best
chess player I've ever seen.

494
00:43:36.948 --> 00:43:39.282
He's also got severe
problems in his head.

495
00:43:39.284 --> 00:43:41.718
Which need to be
understood and managed.

496
00:43:41.720 --> 00:43:43.286
Managed? Bobby?

497
00:43:45.789 --> 00:43:48.224
You ever hear of Paul morphy?

498
00:43:48.226 --> 00:43:49.959
1855.

499
00:43:49.961 --> 00:43:51.461
Greatest player
this country ever had.

500
00:43:51.463 --> 00:43:53.429
Before Bobby, that is.

501
00:43:53.431 --> 00:43:56.165
But by 21,

502
00:43:56.167 --> 00:43:59.135
he'd beat every master
in Europe.

503
00:43:59.137 --> 00:44:02.071
But then...

504
00:44:02.073 --> 00:44:04.741
Things started to get
a little weird.

505
00:44:04.743 --> 00:44:07.310
He started having visions

506
00:44:07.312 --> 00:44:11.014
and became convinced that people
were trying to poison him,

507
00:44:11.016 --> 00:44:12.515
and he quit...

508
00:44:13.417 --> 00:44:15.518
At 26.

509
00:44:15.520 --> 00:44:19.288
And eventually killed himself

510
00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:22.225
in a bathtub surrounded by
12 pairs of ladies' shoes.

511
00:44:24.128 --> 00:44:26.396
So... this game...

512
00:44:31.769 --> 00:44:33.269
it's a rabbit hole.

513
00:44:36.340 --> 00:44:38.107
After only four moves,

514
00:44:38.109 --> 00:44:40.777
there's more than 300 billion
options to consider.

515
00:44:40.779 --> 00:44:45.148
There's more 40-move games than
there are stars in the galaxy.

516
00:44:45.150 --> 00:44:48.685
So... it can take you
very close to the edge.

517
00:44:57.361 --> 00:44:59.128
Could he win?

518
00:45:03.333 --> 00:45:05.501
If-if Bobby were
to go for the title,

519
00:45:05.503 --> 00:45:09.305
it would mean traveling around the world
playing tournaments for three years.

520
00:45:09.307 --> 00:45:11.674
In my opinion, he might
not make it that long.

521
00:45:11.676 --> 00:45:13.242
It's your job
to see that he does.

522
00:45:18.749 --> 00:45:21.217
If we can ever find him.

523
00:45:55.352 --> 00:45:57.120
Holy shit.

524
00:45:58.388 --> 00:46:00.089
They following me?

525
00:46:01.959 --> 00:46:03.192
Hey!

526
00:46:03.194 --> 00:46:06.129
Hey! Hey!

527
00:46:06.131 --> 00:46:09.766
Hey! Over here!
You guys following me?

528
00:46:10.734 --> 00:46:12.735
Hey!

529
00:46:12.737 --> 00:46:15.304
You're soft! You got your guys!

530
00:46:15.306 --> 00:46:17.673
You're a stooge! I got nothing!

531
00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:22.245
Hey! Where you going?

532
00:46:22.247 --> 00:46:23.880
I'm talking to you!

533
00:46:25.082 --> 00:46:26.649
Hey! Where you going?

534
00:46:29.052 --> 00:46:31.220
I'm coming for you!

535
00:46:31.222 --> 00:46:33.489
I'm coming for you!

536
00:46:38.595 --> 00:46:40.396
<i>This is</i> BBC news.

537
00:46:40.398 --> 00:46:42.498
<i>Today in Hungary,</i>

538
00:46:42.500 --> 00:46:46.402
<i>American Bobby Fischer returned
to international competition</i>

539
00:46:46.404 --> 00:46:49.472
<i>by defeating the number-five player
in the world, Viktor Korchnoi,</i>

540
00:46:49.474 --> 00:46:52.408
<i>and declared his intention
to become world champion.</i>

541
00:46:55.580 --> 00:46:59.315
<i>And so begins the grueling challenge
of defeating the grandmasters</i>

542
00:46:59.317 --> 00:47:04.120
<i>who stand between Fischer and the
newly-crowned world champion, Boris Spassky.</i>

543
00:47:04.122 --> 00:47:06.522
Bobby, you dominated
the competition.

544
00:47:06.524 --> 00:47:09.091
How do you feel? I feel good.

545
00:47:09.093 --> 00:47:12.161
Uh, you know, these... these guys
didn't offer me much competition.

546
00:47:13.931 --> 00:47:17.834
<i>I see this as an obstacle course
to the world championship,</i>

547
00:47:17.836 --> 00:47:20.536
and then, uh... then they'll have
to agree to the conditions I want.

548
00:47:20.538 --> 00:47:22.605
Over here, Bobby.

549
00:47:22.607 --> 00:47:24.601
You think the Russians
are worried about you?

550
00:47:24.627 --> 00:47:25.208
Oh, yeah.

551
00:47:25.210 --> 00:47:28.611
They've been worried about me ever
since I started playing chess.

552
00:47:28.613 --> 00:47:30.313
You know, I'm kinda picking
these guys off,

553
00:47:30.315 --> 00:47:32.215
but, uh, they've
been preparing for me,

554
00:47:32.217 --> 00:47:34.350
so you never know what's up their sleeve.
Bobby, over here!

555
00:47:34.352 --> 00:47:36.352
<i>Think anybody can beat you?</i>

556
00:47:54.538 --> 00:47:57.807
<i>The tide of history
is changing, friend.</i>

557
00:47:57.809 --> 00:48:00.409
<i>God's plan
has left them behind,</i>

558
00:48:00.411 --> 00:48:03.546
<i>so they plot to take
the world away from god.</i>

559
00:48:03.548 --> 00:48:06.849
<i>They seek to destroy
our great Christian nation.</i>

560
00:48:06.851 --> 00:48:10.920
<i>As it is written in revelations
18, he will use one nation</i>

561
00:48:10.922 --> 00:48:13.089
what are you listening to?
<i>To punish another nation.</i>

562
00:48:13.091 --> 00:48:14.824
<i>He will take vengeance
on this modern devil.</i>

563
00:48:14.826 --> 00:48:16.726
You mind closing the door?

564
00:48:16.728 --> 00:48:20.263
<i>And they will do anything in
their power to destroy us.</i> Sure.

565
00:48:20.265 --> 00:48:23.599
<i>Theirs is
an international conspiracy.</i>

566
00:48:23.601 --> 00:48:26.269
<i>Mossad, kgb,</i>

567
00:48:26.271 --> 00:48:29.071
<i>the military industrial complex</i>

568
00:48:29.073 --> 00:48:31.374
<i>and an unholy cabal
of Jewish banks.</i>

569
00:48:31.376 --> 00:48:32.942
Joanie, joanie, joanie.

570
00:48:32.944 --> 00:48:35.544
I'm between
three airports and hell.

571
00:48:35.546 --> 00:48:39.081
I have 10 minutes in New York to see
my wife, kiss my kids, talk to you.

572
00:48:39.083 --> 00:48:41.684
How the hell you been?
You're looking great.

573
00:48:41.686 --> 00:48:43.786
I have to see him.

574
00:48:43.788 --> 00:48:45.788
Every time I call, they
say his phone is broken.

575
00:48:47.758 --> 00:48:50.259
- That happens.
- You mean he breaks them.

576
00:48:53.730 --> 00:48:55.698
I wanna take him
to see a doctor.

577
00:48:55.700 --> 00:48:59.235
Ah, well, that just
won't be possible.

578
00:48:59.237 --> 00:49:01.137
He's already in Vancouver.

579
00:49:02.139 --> 00:49:03.873
These.

580
00:49:03.875 --> 00:49:06.208
Every week,

581
00:49:06.210 --> 00:49:08.411
his letters, they...

582
00:49:08.413 --> 00:49:10.780
they get more cra...

583
00:49:10.782 --> 00:49:12.949
more crazy. Take a look.
Go ahead.

584
00:49:12.951 --> 00:49:15.918
Um, they're private letters
to you, Joan.

585
00:49:15.920 --> 00:49:17.887
I, um...

586
00:49:17.889 --> 00:49:19.789
I respect his privacy.

587
00:49:21.058 --> 00:49:23.893
I showed them to
a psychiatrist friend.

588
00:49:23.895 --> 00:49:27.563
He said that Bobby is
displaying signs of, um...

589
00:49:27.565 --> 00:49:32.568
uh... of paranoia
and delusional psychosis.

590
00:49:32.570 --> 00:49:36.906
Well, you know, uh,
chess is a crazy world.

591
00:49:36.908 --> 00:49:39.842
Some of the things he says
about the Soviets are true.

592
00:49:39.844 --> 00:49:43.179
In Tunis, we found listening
devices in his hotel room.

593
00:49:48.118 --> 00:49:50.820
"The communists..."

594
00:49:50.822 --> 00:49:53.789
Infect my mind with words
that just keep repeating.

595
00:49:53.791 --> 00:49:56.926
The Jews are helping them too.

596
00:49:56.928 --> 00:49:59.695
The Jews wanna keep the chess
federation all to themselves

597
00:49:59.697 --> 00:50:01.430
just like they own New York

598
00:50:01.432 --> 00:50:03.432
"and own and control most
governments in the world."

599
00:50:03.434 --> 00:50:06.435
We... we... we are Jewish.

600
00:50:07.037 --> 00:50:08.871
Bobby is Jewish.

601
00:50:08.873 --> 00:50:11.207
What do you people say to him when
he comes out with this trash?

602
00:50:11.209 --> 00:50:16.345
He doesn't listen to me.
He doesn't listen to anybody.

603
00:50:16.347 --> 00:50:19.448
Joanie, look, I swear, this isn't
just about the title anymore.

604
00:50:19.450 --> 00:50:21.684
It's about the kind
of games he's playing.

605
00:50:21.686 --> 00:50:24.220
Out of all the crazy stuff,

606
00:50:24.222 --> 00:50:29.291
such... Unimaginable beauty.

607
00:50:29.293 --> 00:50:32.461
A Da Vinci, they're
saying, from Brooklyn.

608
00:50:32.463 --> 00:50:35.531
Once every what, 500 years?

609
00:50:35.533 --> 00:50:40.936
Grandmasters are watching Bobby
play with tears in their eyes.

610
00:50:51.815 --> 00:50:53.549
Joanie, look,
I'll take care of him.

611
00:50:53.551 --> 00:50:55.584
Okay? I'll take care of him.

612
00:50:57.387 --> 00:51:01.223
I'll make sure his life
doesn't get too crazy.

613
00:51:01.225 --> 00:51:04.160
There he is! There he is!
Bobby, right here.

614
00:51:04.162 --> 00:51:07.082
You've never beaten Spassky before. Why
do you think you can beat him now?

615
00:51:12.536 --> 00:51:14.737
Bobby, look at me.

616
00:51:14.739 --> 00:51:17.173
<i>This is</i> BBC news.

617
00:51:17.175 --> 00:51:21.043
<i>Today, Bobby Fischer beat Russian
grandmaster Tigran Petrosian...</i>

618
00:51:21.045 --> 00:51:23.679
<i>In the semifinal of the
world chess championships.</i>

619
00:51:23.681 --> 00:51:26.415
<i>Fischer will now face the current
world champion, Boris Spassky.</i>

620
00:51:26.417 --> 00:51:29.251
Bobby, with this win you're guaranteed
a shot at the championship.

621
00:51:29.253 --> 00:51:32.021
How does it feel? It's about time.
I always said I was the best.

622
00:51:32.023 --> 00:51:34.356
Everyone said, "oh, he's an arrogant,
terrible, conceited person."

623
00:51:34.358 --> 00:51:36.271
But now it's just an
obvious fact I'm the best.

624
00:51:36.297 --> 00:51:37.426
You've never beaten Spassky.

625
00:51:37.428 --> 00:51:39.562
The Russians are saying
you don't have a chance.

626
00:51:39.564 --> 00:51:42.231
I don't care two cents about him one way
or the other. He's just another guy.

627
00:51:42.233 --> 00:51:44.100
What's this about the
Russians spying on you?

628
00:51:44.102 --> 00:51:45.968
Oh, yeah, sure. Yeah.
They, uh...

629
00:51:45.970 --> 00:51:48.737
I've seen them following me. They
bugged the phones in my hotel rooms.

630
00:51:48.739 --> 00:51:51.440
I heard they can even listen through
the fillings in your teeth.

631
00:51:51.442 --> 00:51:53.609
- You're kidding, right?
- I'm not kidding. Go see your dentist.

632
00:51:53.611 --> 00:51:55.578
Petrosian said he wasn't well.

633
00:51:55.580 --> 00:51:58.080
He's the second Russian to get sick playing you.
They're all getting sick.

634
00:51:58.082 --> 00:52:01.417
Bobby! Bobby!

635
00:52:07.992 --> 00:52:10.926
<i>Wide world of sports</i> is
already offering a segment.

636
00:52:10.928 --> 00:52:13.629
Yeah. Cavett wants you,
and... are you ready?

637
00:52:13.631 --> 00:52:15.931
Are you sitting down?
Are you sitting down?

638
00:52:15.933 --> 00:52:19.101
Mike Wallace called.
Mike Wallace called.

639
00:52:19.103 --> 00:52:22.338
<i>60 minutes,</i> Bobby.
Well, it's about time.

640
00:52:22.340 --> 00:52:25.007
You know, I don't wanna get distracted
doing a bunch of press though.

641
00:52:25.009 --> 00:52:27.810
No, this is... this is not ordinary
press, Bobby. I know, I know.

642
00:52:27.812 --> 00:52:30.446
I'll-I'll do the big ones.
Great. That is great.

643
00:52:30.448 --> 00:52:32.281
Only, um... hey, I'm starving.
Let's get some food.

644
00:52:32.283 --> 00:52:33.849
When we do these shows, yeah.

645
00:52:33.851 --> 00:52:35.684
Just go easy on the
bugging and the teeth.

646
00:52:35.686 --> 00:52:37.353
What are you talking about?

647
00:52:37.355 --> 00:52:39.255
I'm just... I'm just
saying what's on my mind.

648
00:52:39.257 --> 00:52:40.990
I know. I know.
I'm telling the truth.

649
00:52:40.992 --> 00:52:42.858
There's nothing wrong with
what I'm saying. I know.

650
00:52:42.860 --> 00:52:44.793
And people like that.
You need to be careful.

651
00:52:44.795 --> 00:52:46.495
Some things you say,
they can be misinterpreted.

652
00:52:46.497 --> 00:52:48.164
People get worried
when you talk like that.

653
00:52:48.166 --> 00:52:49.832
What are you saying?
"People get worried."

654
00:52:49.834 --> 00:52:51.467
Who? What people? No one.

655
00:52:51.469 --> 00:52:53.135
You're talking about yourself.
No one.

656
00:52:53.137 --> 00:52:55.838
Which of these interviews do you wanna do?
Now, wait a second.

657
00:52:55.840 --> 00:52:58.674
What did you mean, "people begin to worry"?
What are you talking about?

658
00:52:58.676 --> 00:53:00.809
Doesn't matter. I think
cavett is a great idea.

659
00:53:00.811 --> 00:53:02.945
Why do you keep changing the subject?
I'm not changing...

660
00:53:02.947 --> 00:53:05.628
I'm asking you a question. You said
people get worried. What people?

661
00:53:08.718 --> 00:53:10.686
Uh, Joan.

662
00:53:10.688 --> 00:53:12.955
Joan? My sister? How would you know that?
Did you talk to her?

663
00:53:12.957 --> 00:53:14.890
I was in New York.

664
00:53:16.326 --> 00:53:19.862
We, um... we had
a cup of coffee.

665
00:53:19.864 --> 00:53:23.299
You met with my sister? Without telling me?
Wha... why would you do that?

666
00:53:23.301 --> 00:53:25.501
She called me. What were
you guys talking about?

667
00:53:25.503 --> 00:53:27.236
Were you talking about me?

668
00:53:27.238 --> 00:53:28.918
Well, I was looking out
for your interests.

669
00:53:30.574 --> 00:53:32.408
That's my job.
Don't lawyer me, Paul.

670
00:53:32.410 --> 00:53:34.043
I'm not trying to...
don't lawyer me.

671
00:53:34.045 --> 00:53:36.145
You're talking with my
family behind my back.

672
00:53:36.147 --> 00:53:38.247
Did I ask you to do that?

673
00:53:38.249 --> 00:53:39.915
What were you doing?
You're spying on me?

674
00:53:39.917 --> 00:53:41.584
I was trying
to help you, Bobby.

675
00:53:41.586 --> 00:53:43.719
I don't understand why you
thought that was a good idea.

676
00:53:43.721 --> 00:53:46.422
There are a hundred more of
you just right behind you,

677
00:53:46.424 --> 00:53:48.390
waiting in line to be here.

678
00:53:52.696 --> 00:53:55.064
Hey, pull over. Um...

679
00:53:55.066 --> 00:53:57.199
- stop the car.
- Wait a minute. Bobby. Bobby.

680
00:53:57.201 --> 00:53:59.168
We're about to get everything
you've ever wanted.

681
00:53:59.170 --> 00:54:02.238
Stop the car! Pull over!
Stop the car now!

682
00:54:16.219 --> 00:54:18.320
Bishop, bishop six.

683
00:54:19.589 --> 00:54:22.524
Uh, king to queen four.

684
00:54:23.627 --> 00:54:25.160
Rook, rook five.

685
00:54:26.529 --> 00:54:27.963
Pawn to king five.

686
00:54:43.747 --> 00:54:44.780
So?

687
00:54:49.386 --> 00:54:52.488
- He's back in his room.
- Is he okay?

688
00:54:52.490 --> 00:54:54.990
He's in there listening
to those tapes of his.

689
00:54:59.829 --> 00:55:03.732
Isn't that, uh,
some kind of god squad?

690
00:55:03.734 --> 00:55:06.201
Those people know
as much about god as you do.

691
00:55:07.570 --> 00:55:09.505
I'd have thought you'd approve.

692
00:55:13.910 --> 00:55:16.612
Should we try to get him to see someone?
A psychiatrist?

693
00:55:16.614 --> 00:55:19.114
Never gonna happen.

694
00:55:19.116 --> 00:55:22.451
Concierge told me he asked to
have the TV removed from his room

695
00:55:22.453 --> 00:55:25.053
'cause he thinks the Russians are
watching him through the screen.

696
00:55:25.422 --> 00:55:27.022
Maybe they are.

697
00:55:29.626 --> 00:55:34.396
He also believes they're gonna
try to blow up his plane.

698
00:55:36.533 --> 00:55:38.667
What good would a doctor do?

699
00:55:38.669 --> 00:55:41.270
Give him a diagnosis. Hmm.

700
00:55:41.272 --> 00:55:44.106
Uh, give him a pill.

701
00:55:44.108 --> 00:55:47.409
That would be like pouring
concrete down a holy well.

702
00:55:52.849 --> 00:55:55.150
<i>Bobby Fischer, the United
States chess champion,</i>

703
00:55:55.152 --> 00:55:58.887
<i>has challenged world champion Boris
Spassky in a 24-game series.</i>

704
00:55:58.889 --> 00:56:01.290
Playing Boris Spassky.
Are you afraid?

705
00:56:01.292 --> 00:56:03.459
I'm not afraid of him.
He's afraid of me.

706
00:56:03.461 --> 00:56:07.229
Last time we played, a couple
people said they saw him trembling.

707
00:56:07.231 --> 00:56:09.365
<i>Fischer, the first
American in history</i>

708
00:56:09.367 --> 00:56:11.800
<i>to reach the finals
of the world championship,</i>

709
00:56:11.802 --> 00:56:15.204
<i>is the latest arrival in our
growing culture of celebrity.</i>

710
00:56:15.206 --> 00:56:17.172
<i>But who would have thought
that a chess player</i>

711
00:56:17.174 --> 00:56:19.575
<i>could suddenly become
a rock star?</i>

712
00:56:19.577 --> 00:56:23.245
<i>My next guest is prepared to take
on the Russians single-handed.</i>

713
00:56:23.247 --> 00:56:25.047
<i>And he may win.</i>

714
00:56:25.049 --> 00:56:27.816
<i>Will you welcome a bright boy
from Brooklyn, Mr. Bobby Fischer?</i>

715
00:56:30.221 --> 00:56:32.254
<i>All right.</i>

716
00:56:32.256 --> 00:56:35.491
<i>Do you honestly think that you probably
are the world's greatest chess player?</i>

717
00:56:35.493 --> 00:56:37.059
Yeah. Yeah, right.

718
00:56:38.763 --> 00:56:40.429
You know
what<i> life</i> magazine said.

719
00:56:40.431 --> 00:56:42.931
"Bobby Fischer is single-handedly
changing the face of chess,

720
00:56:42.933 --> 00:56:44.566
<i>making it
the fastest-growing sport..."</i>

721
00:56:44.568 --> 00:56:46.335
<i>And what's the moment
of pleasure for you?</i>

722
00:56:46.337 --> 00:56:48.203
<i>Is it when you see
the guy in trouble?</i>

723
00:56:48.205 --> 00:56:49.938
<i>Where's the greatest pleasure?</i>

724
00:56:49.940 --> 00:56:52.408
<i>What would correspond to hitting
the home run in baseball?</i>

725
00:56:52.410 --> 00:56:54.843
<i>Greatest pleasure?</i>

726
00:56:54.845 --> 00:56:57.613
<i>When you break his ego.
That's where it's at.</i>

727
00:56:57.615 --> 00:57:00.115
<i>- Really?
- Yeah.</i>

728
00:57:00.117 --> 00:57:01.884
<i>And when does that occur?</i>

729
00:57:01.886 --> 00:57:03.752
<i>When he sees
that he's finished?</i>

730
00:57:03.754 --> 00:57:06.922
<i>When he sees it coming
and breaks all up inside.</i>

731
00:57:08.591 --> 00:57:12.594
<i>Their goal is nothing short
of world domination.</i>

732
00:57:12.596 --> 00:57:16.398
<i>And unless they are stopped, that
goal soon will be within their grasp.</i>

733
00:57:16.400 --> 00:57:19.234
<i>Look at the godless
Soviet union</i>

734
00:57:19.236 --> 00:57:22.070
<i>and their relentless march
across eastern Europe.</i>

735
00:57:22.072 --> 00:57:24.273
<i>Look at the illegitimate
state of Israel</i>

736
00:57:24.275 --> 00:57:28.811
<i>whose expansionist ambitions have
usurped palestine from its true heirs</i>

737
00:57:28.813 --> 00:57:31.079
<i>in the kingdom of Judah.</i>

738
00:57:31.081 --> 00:57:34.650
<i>Indeed, the people of Israel
are not even the real Jews.</i>

739
00:57:34.652 --> 00:57:39.655
<i>That tribe was taken captive by king
nebuchadnezzar of Babylon in 600 be.</i>

740
00:57:56.974 --> 00:57:59.408
Bobby, turn the music down!

741
00:57:59.410 --> 00:58:01.677
He doesn't want the Russians to
know what plane we're taking.

742
00:58:01.679 --> 00:58:04.413
And I want the food to travel
with us on the plane, Paul!

743
00:58:04.415 --> 00:58:06.615
You got it, Bobby!
We have some issues.

744
00:58:06.617 --> 00:58:09.751
Well, the plane leaves in two hours.
With the traffic...

745
00:58:09.753 --> 00:58:11.820
- and make sure there's enough oranges!
- Check!

746
00:58:11.822 --> 00:58:14.289
There's never enough oranges!
Uh-huh.

747
00:58:14.291 --> 00:58:16.758
He wants all the food flown in
and prepared in front of him.

748
00:58:16.760 --> 00:58:18.760
Okay.

749
00:58:18.762 --> 00:58:24.032
He also wants $46,875 in his
bank account before he plays.

750
00:58:24.034 --> 00:58:25.868
He'll pick up the rest
when he wins.

751
00:58:25.870 --> 00:58:27.803
Well, that's not
going to happen.

752
00:58:27.805 --> 00:58:29.638
Yeah, otherwise he's not going.

753
00:58:29.640 --> 00:58:32.040
Which is what he wants anyway.

754
00:58:32.042 --> 00:58:34.643
What are you guys
whispering about in there?

755
00:58:34.645 --> 00:58:37.679
Just talking travel plans, Bobby.
Who's traveling?

756
00:58:37.681 --> 00:58:41.216
You're really serious
about all this?

757
00:58:42.285 --> 00:58:43.852
They gotta give me what I want.

758
00:58:43.854 --> 00:58:45.687
Mm-hmm. And if they don't?

759
00:58:45.689 --> 00:58:48.490
Well, we can play in this hotel room.
I like it here.

760
00:58:48.492 --> 00:58:51.126
Let me get this straight. You're willing to
throw all this away for money and oranges?

761
00:58:51.128 --> 00:58:53.529
That's right. I don't need to play.
I know I'm the best.

762
00:58:53.531 --> 00:58:55.397
I think you're scared.

763
00:58:56.499 --> 00:58:58.700
And I think you're overwhelmed.

764
00:58:58.702 --> 00:59:00.802
Is that, like, um,
pop psychology?

765
00:59:04.307 --> 00:59:05.774
Yeah.

766
00:59:08.344 --> 00:59:11.046
You've got the passports, right?
You've got them? Yeah.

767
00:59:11.048 --> 00:59:14.683
I want my passport.

768
00:59:14.685 --> 00:59:19.421
You... you're not leaving
for a half an hour. Yeah.

769
00:59:19.423 --> 00:59:21.523
Yeah. Maybe I'll take
a later flight. Or not.

770
00:59:21.525 --> 00:59:22.525
Okay.

771
00:59:23.560 --> 00:59:24.560
Okay.

772
00:59:26.496 --> 00:59:28.897
That's kind of funny,
seeing a priest get mad.

773
00:59:28.899 --> 00:59:31.567
I'm getting on the phone
right now.

774
00:59:31.569 --> 00:59:35.003
I'm gonna make this work.
I don't mind either way.

775
00:59:35.005 --> 00:59:36.438
I really got nothing to prove.

776
01:00:13.978 --> 01:00:16.339
Well, what's the issue, Bobby?
I don't know.

777
01:00:16.365 --> 01:00:17.571
Something's not right.

778
01:00:27.591 --> 01:00:29.758
We settled too low.
I oughta get 30%.

779
01:00:29.760 --> 01:00:31.827
Bobby, we agreed on 25%.

780
01:00:31.829 --> 01:00:34.496
Well, it's 30 now. We agreed.

781
01:00:34.498 --> 01:00:37.432
Well, I changed my mind, Paul.
I understand that.

782
01:00:37.434 --> 01:00:39.968
But, Bobby, you just can't
change your mind like that.

783
01:00:39.970 --> 01:00:42.671
We're<i> here.</i> We're at the airport.
What am I supposed to do?

784
01:00:42.673 --> 01:00:44.406
I don't have to get on the plane.
I don't know.

785
01:00:44.408 --> 01:00:46.008
You're not gonna get on the plane?
Now?

786
01:00:47.444 --> 01:00:50.112
What is this, Paul? I told you!
No photographers!

787
01:00:50.114 --> 01:00:52.014
Move it! Move out of the way!
All right, fine.

788
01:00:52.016 --> 01:00:54.783
Bobby! Bobby!

789
01:01:06.295 --> 01:01:09.464
Where to? Just go! Drive!

790
01:01:16.106 --> 01:01:19.174
<i>Another bizarre turn today in
the world chess championships</i>

791
01:01:19.176 --> 01:01:22.477
<i>when Bobby Fischer failed to
appear for the opening ceremonies.</i>

792
01:01:22.479 --> 01:01:25.781
<i>Meanwhile, Bobby's opponent, Boris
Spassky, arrived in Reykjavik today</i>

793
01:01:25.783 --> 01:01:28.717
<i>to the excitement
of all of Iceland.</i>

794
01:01:28.719 --> 01:01:31.486
<i>Three-day adjudicator Wolfgang
Schmid, had this to say.</i>

795
01:01:31.488 --> 01:01:34.723
I am honored, but confused.

796
01:01:34.725 --> 01:01:38.293
I was always led to believe that
chess needed two people for a game.

797
01:01:38.295 --> 01:01:41.329
Unfortunately,
our American friends

798
01:01:41.331 --> 01:01:43.665
have managed to lose
a famous Mr. Bobby Fischer.

799
01:01:45.703 --> 01:01:49.538
One hopes they are not so careless
with their nuclear weapons.

800
01:01:49.540 --> 01:01:53.375
Mr. Tremblay, is Bobby not showing
affecting Iceland relationships?

801
01:01:53.377 --> 01:01:55.977
Is it true he denied two million
dollars to come to Reykjavik?

802
01:01:55.979 --> 01:01:58.080
I love chess.
The world loves chess.

803
01:01:58.082 --> 01:02:00.315
We are here to celebrate chess.
Excuse us, please.

804
01:02:00.317 --> 01:02:02.084
<i>Still no sign of Bobby Fischer,</i>

805
01:02:02.086 --> 01:02:05.187
<i>the American chess player who
thinks he's the best in the world.</i>

806
01:02:05.189 --> 01:02:07.556
<i>Fischer today was supposed to
be in Reykjavik, Iceland</i>

807
01:02:07.558 --> 01:02:10.425
<i>to start playing Boris Spassky
of the Soviet union</i>

808
01:02:10.427 --> 01:02:13.095
<i>in what promises to be the
super bowl of the chess world,</i>

809
01:02:13.097 --> 01:02:15.564
<i>if it ever comes off,
and that's a big if.</i>

810
01:02:15.566 --> 01:02:18.700
<i>Fischer is still believed to be in New York
City, where he was last seen in public...</i>

811
01:02:18.702 --> 01:02:20.268
Mama? Yeah, baby.

812
01:02:20.270 --> 01:02:22.871
There are men on the lawn.

813
01:02:22.873 --> 01:02:25.074
<i>Today the international chess
federation said</i> come here.

814
01:02:25.075 --> 01:02:29.277
<i>If Fischer is not in Iceland by noon
on Tuesday, he will be disqualified.</i>

815
01:02:29.279 --> 01:02:32.614
<i>In other news, the white house refused
to comment on the recent break-in</i>

816
01:02:32.616 --> 01:02:35.450
<i>at the Watergate headquarters of
the Democratic national committee.</i>

817
01:02:43.960 --> 01:02:46.094
Hello? Hello?

818
01:02:47.263 --> 01:02:50.198
Bobby, is that you?

819
01:02:51.701 --> 01:02:54.770
Yeah. I, uh...

820
01:02:54.772 --> 01:02:57.272
they promised me no photographers
and a bunch of them showed up,

821
01:02:57.274 --> 01:02:59.040
so I had to get out of there.

822
01:02:59.042 --> 01:03:02.244
<i>Where are you?</i> I'm-I'm here.
I'm at home.

823
01:03:02.246 --> 01:03:05.781
<i>I was going to the airport, and
they promised no photographers,</i>

824
01:03:05.783 --> 01:03:08.316
and a bunch of them showed up,
so I had to get out of there.

825
01:03:08.318 --> 01:03:10.652
I had to show them, Joan.

826
01:03:10.654 --> 01:03:13.088
They told me no photographers.

827
01:03:13.090 --> 01:03:17.125
They keep doing this to me,
everywhere I go, all the time.

828
01:03:17.127 --> 01:03:19.127
People keep doing this to me.
<i>Yeah.</i>

829
01:03:19.129 --> 01:03:20.328
It's not fair.

830
01:03:20.330 --> 01:03:23.398
Bobby, are you okay, huh?

831
01:03:23.400 --> 01:03:25.567
<i>Well, you know that 727
that went down?</i>

832
01:03:25.569 --> 01:03:29.237
You know, they said
the fuel tank exploded.

833
01:03:29.239 --> 01:03:32.007
Yeah, well that seemed kind
of funny to me. Right?

834
01:03:32.009 --> 01:03:34.142
<i>You know, they don't
tell us everything.</i>

835
01:03:34.144 --> 01:03:36.478
Yeah, no, they don't.
That's true.

836
01:03:36.480 --> 01:03:39.481
<i>And the kgb, they kill people.
They do.</i>

837
01:03:39.483 --> 01:03:43.685
<i>Th-th-that's real. They put poison on the
tips of their umbrellas, crazy things.</i>

838
01:03:43.687 --> 01:03:46.521
<i>You understand?</i> Yeah.

839
01:03:46.523 --> 01:03:49.157
Baby, you stay there, okay?
I'm going to come over.

840
01:03:49.159 --> 01:03:53.028
These people, I wouldn't
put anything past them.

841
01:03:53.030 --> 01:03:56.498
Who-who people, Bobby? <i>The Russians.
You believe me.</i>

842
01:03:56.500 --> 01:03:59.334
They would like it
if I dropped dead tonight.

843
01:04:00.503 --> 01:04:02.671
You hear that? Shh, shh, shh!

844
01:04:02.673 --> 01:04:05.040
<i>Do I hear what?</i>

845
01:04:05.042 --> 01:04:08.810
Shh.

846
01:04:08.812 --> 01:04:10.879
I don't know. There's
a noise on the phone.

847
01:04:10.881 --> 01:04:12.848
Do you hear it?
<i>No, I don't hear anything.</i>

848
01:04:14.851 --> 01:04:15.851
Bobby?

849
01:04:20.489 --> 01:04:25.360
We have a NATO base out there, and I
asked for the marines to guard me.

850
01:04:25.362 --> 01:04:28.063
They said<i> no</i> to me.
They said no, Joan.

851
01:04:28.065 --> 01:04:31.800
With all the prestige I am
bringing to this country.

852
01:04:31.802 --> 01:04:33.401
I'm the most famous man
in the world.

853
01:04:33.403 --> 01:04:35.837
Do you hear that?

854
01:04:35.839 --> 01:04:41.576
<i>They put stuff in my food to
mess with my mind, you know?</i>

855
01:04:41.578 --> 01:04:45.180
I don't know. <i>Bobby, you stay there.
I'm-I'm gonna come over.</i>

856
01:04:45.182 --> 01:04:49.384
No, no, no, no, no. Listen.
Joan, listen to me.

857
01:04:49.386 --> 01:04:51.920
I'm trying to
tell you something.

858
01:04:51.922 --> 01:04:54.756
I keep... I keep
thinking about this.

859
01:04:54.758 --> 01:04:59.094
<i>I keep going over it and over
it and over it in my mind.</i>

860
01:04:59.096 --> 01:05:02.397
I can't figure out
what's next, Joan.

861
01:05:04.200 --> 01:05:05.934
Do you know what I mean?
<i>Yeah. Yeah.</i>

862
01:05:05.936 --> 01:05:08.637
Do you understand? <i>Yeah. Yeah.
I understand, baby. Yeah.</i>

863
01:05:12.608 --> 01:05:15.443
- Bobby...
- Shh! Joan.

864
01:05:15.445 --> 01:05:18.580
<i>Goddamn it. Do you hear that?</i>

865
01:05:24.420 --> 01:05:26.421
There's a noise.

866
01:05:27.256 --> 01:05:29.424
Joan.

867
01:05:32.696 --> 01:05:34.062
I'm gonna get off the phone.

868
01:05:34.064 --> 01:05:36.097
Bobby, you...

869
01:05:48.044 --> 01:05:50.378
uh, who's there?

870
01:05:50.380 --> 01:05:51.713
Hey, pal, you in there?

871
01:05:54.317 --> 01:05:58.053
Hey, can I come in? Got big news.
Yeah, what's that?

872
01:05:58.055 --> 01:06:01.656
You wanted more money? I
just got you an extra 130k.

873
01:06:01.658 --> 01:06:05.160
Some English chess nut
wants to see you play so bad.

874
01:06:06.562 --> 01:06:09.664
Is the money real?
It's real. It's good.

875
01:06:09.666 --> 01:06:11.599
It's secure.

876
01:06:11.601 --> 01:06:14.002
May I use your phone?
Let folks know you're okay?

877
01:06:17.239 --> 01:06:19.341
- Hey, who's that?
- That's, uh, security.

878
01:06:19.343 --> 01:06:21.476
They've fixed it for us at the airport.
We can go straight to the plane.

879
01:06:21.478 --> 01:06:23.211
No press.

880
01:06:26.248 --> 01:06:28.650
Yes. Hello?

881
01:06:28.652 --> 01:06:31.152
I found him.

882
01:06:31.154 --> 01:06:32.721
It's Henry kissinger.

883
01:06:35.257 --> 01:06:37.092
Pretty amazing, huh?

884
01:06:40.429 --> 01:06:42.697
Hello?
<i>Dr. Kissinger's on the line.</i>

885
01:06:44.367 --> 01:06:47.168
<i>Hello. This is the worst
chess player in the world</i>

886
01:06:47.170 --> 01:06:50.305
<i>calling the best chess player
in the world.</i>

887
01:06:50.307 --> 01:06:53.041
<i>We just want you to know,
the president and I,</i>

888
01:06:53.043 --> 01:06:56.845
<i>that we are thrilled that you will be going
to Iceland to play for the usa, Bobby.</i>

889
01:06:58.881 --> 01:07:02.751
Yeah. Yeah.
I, uh... I decided to play.

890
01:07:06.989 --> 01:07:09.724
<i>This is the BBC world service.</i>

891
01:07:09.726 --> 01:07:12.494
<i>American chess grandmaster,
Bobby Fischer,</i>

892
01:07:12.496 --> 01:07:16.231
<i>is believed to be finally on his way to
Iceland for his match with Boris Spassky.</i>

893
01:07:26.642 --> 01:07:28.977
This is how we used to
get Jagger out of here.

894
01:07:28.979 --> 01:07:31.479
<i>After months of negotiation,</i>

895
01:07:31.481 --> 01:07:34.215
<i>Fischer will now face the current
world champion, Boris Spassky.</i>

896
01:07:34.217 --> 01:07:37.085
What do you think of Bobby?
I don't know.

897
01:07:37.087 --> 01:07:40.688
Uh, least there's one reason
to feel good about America.

898
01:07:40.690 --> 01:07:41.555
A bit impolite.

899
01:07:41.581 --> 01:07:44.325
Fischer is to chess
like Ali is to boxing.

900
01:07:44.327 --> 01:07:47.896
<i>He's tedious. He's arrogant.
He's inconsiderate.</i>

901
01:07:47.898 --> 01:07:50.765
<i>At long last, Bobby Fischer
arrived in Reykjavik today</i>

902
01:07:50.767 --> 01:07:53.701
<i>after months of speculation
that he wouldn't appear at all.</i>

903
01:07:53.703 --> 01:07:56.704
Yeah, well the Russians have been telling
lies about me since I was a kid.

904
01:07:56.706 --> 01:08:00.375
- How do you like Iceland?
- Iceland? Yeah, I'm happy to be here.

905
01:08:00.377 --> 01:08:02.143
I feel like a viking,
ready to conquer.

906
01:08:18.961 --> 01:08:21.763
Come in. Take a look around.

907
01:08:21.765 --> 01:08:23.932
Yeah. There's good.

908
01:08:23.934 --> 01:08:26.534
It's, uh, five bedrooms.

909
01:08:27.536 --> 01:08:29.370
Place even has a sauna.

910
01:08:29.372 --> 01:08:31.372
All right. I'm gonna check out
the rooms, see which one I want.

911
01:08:31.374 --> 01:08:33.308
Go for it.

912
01:08:38.747 --> 01:08:40.415
Yeah.

913
01:08:43.954 --> 01:08:49.023
Huh. Hey, uh,
what are you doing here?

914
01:08:54.130 --> 01:08:55.563
I took a little time.

915
01:08:57.233 --> 01:08:58.366
All right.

916
01:09:02.338 --> 01:09:05.173
You want to work? Want to
analyze some positions?

917
01:09:05.175 --> 01:09:08.610
Nah, maybe later.
What's wrong? You sick?

918
01:09:08.612 --> 01:09:10.478
I'm just taking a break.

919
01:09:12.448 --> 01:09:15.183
Okay. I'll check up
on you in a few hours.

920
01:09:16.018 --> 01:09:18.086
It's good to see you, Bobby.

921
01:09:19.822 --> 01:09:22.257
Yeah, sure.

922
01:09:25.694 --> 01:09:28.463
Couldn't stay away, huh?

923
01:09:28.465 --> 01:09:30.765
Well, I get it.

924
01:09:30.767 --> 01:09:33.768
Yankees are playing in the series,
you want a seat in the dugout.

925
01:09:33.770 --> 01:09:35.670
Can I get one of these?

926
01:09:39.308 --> 01:09:42.544
Billion people around the world
watching two guys play chess.

927
01:09:43.979 --> 01:09:46.447
Nixon's put a TV
in the oval office.

928
01:09:46.449 --> 01:09:48.116
Oh, yeah? Mmm.

929
01:09:48.118 --> 01:09:50.118
World war III on a chess board.

930
01:09:52.888 --> 01:09:54.389
We lost China.

931
01:09:55.791 --> 01:09:58.092
We're losing Vietnam.

932
01:10:00.729 --> 01:10:02.197
We have to win this one.

933
01:10:19.181 --> 01:10:21.583
This is abc's
<i>wide world of sports,</i>

934
01:10:21.585 --> 01:10:24.686
bringing you live coverage of the
world championship of chess.

935
01:10:24.688 --> 01:10:27.155
<i>Best of 24 games.</i>

936
01:10:27.157 --> 01:10:30.525
<i>Each win is worth a point.
Each draw is half a point.</i>

937
01:10:30.527 --> 01:10:34.495
It's the chess version of a
15-round heavyweight prize fight.

938
01:10:36.199 --> 01:10:39.200
And the noise from the crowd
tells us that Boris Spassky...

939
01:10:44.107 --> 01:10:47.108
in what will surely be
an historic confrontation.

940
01:11:03.559 --> 01:11:06.794
Where is he?

941
01:11:44.267 --> 01:11:46.768
If the press could please
take their seats,

942
01:11:46.770 --> 01:11:49.771
we will now come together
for the drawing of the colors.

943
01:11:49.773 --> 01:11:52.407
Mr. Spassky?
Mr. Fischer, choose.

944
01:11:57.112 --> 01:11:59.514
Mr. Fischer chose black.
Mr. Spassky will begin.

945
01:12:48.230 --> 01:12:50.498
So far, so good, yeah?

946
01:12:50.500 --> 01:12:53.534
Mmm. They're playing
a variation on a nimzo.

947
01:12:53.536 --> 01:12:55.536
A draw would be fine
for our side.

948
01:13:13.856 --> 01:13:17.425
Is that bad? No, no. It
simplifies the game.

949
01:14:14.583 --> 01:14:16.751
Your cameras are making too much noise.
It's distracting.

950
01:14:16.753 --> 01:14:17.885
I'll be back.

951
01:14:17.887 --> 01:14:19.454
From the cameras?

952
01:14:19.456 --> 01:14:22.256
Yeah, they're going like...

953
01:14:25.561 --> 01:14:27.361
come here.

954
01:14:32.100 --> 01:14:35.069
Do you hear that?

955
01:14:35.071 --> 01:14:37.839
It's just a camera. Yeah. Well,
you've got to move it back.

956
01:14:37.841 --> 01:14:40.107
What? Move the camera back.

957
01:14:40.109 --> 01:14:42.310
Move it back.
Move the camera back.

958
01:14:42.312 --> 01:14:44.076
You've got to get the
audience under control too.

959
01:14:44.102 --> 01:14:45.346
What's wrong
with the audience?

960
01:14:45.348 --> 01:14:47.415
They're making too much noise,
and you're not doing anything.

961
01:14:47.417 --> 01:14:51.018
That's your job. All right?
Do your job.

962
01:14:51.020 --> 01:14:54.188
Mr. Spassky's made his move.
It's good.

963
01:15:02.197 --> 01:15:04.131
Yes, yes. Herr
Schmid, Mr. Schmid,

964
01:15:04.133 --> 01:15:07.168
he's trying to upset my player.

965
01:15:07.170 --> 01:15:10.037
You see, the camera was making noise.
We just moved it back.

966
01:15:10.039 --> 01:15:12.440
- He is upsetting my player.
- Yes, it's fine.

967
01:15:12.442 --> 01:15:14.041
If you could just
take your seats, please.

968
01:15:14.043 --> 01:15:16.377
Thank you.

969
01:15:17.514 --> 01:15:19.413
Thank you.

970
01:17:20.836 --> 01:17:22.670
You don't look well,
Robert James.

971
01:17:52.000 --> 01:17:55.269
First game
goes to Boris Spassky.

972
01:18:15.824 --> 01:18:18.793
Bobby! Bobby!

973
01:18:18.795 --> 01:18:20.327
- Hold on a second!
- Don't yell at him.

974
01:18:20.329 --> 01:18:21.862
I'm not yelling at him. Bobby!

975
01:18:21.864 --> 01:18:24.632
Bobby!

976
01:18:24.634 --> 01:18:27.368
It's just one game. One point.

977
01:18:27.370 --> 01:18:29.236
You'll take the next one.

978
01:18:29.238 --> 01:18:32.406
Twelve and a half points
is-is a long match.

979
01:18:34.676 --> 01:18:36.811
The reporters
will go after Spassky.

980
01:18:36.813 --> 01:18:38.746
We could slip out another way.

981
01:18:40.382 --> 01:18:42.750
You all right?

982
01:18:42.752 --> 01:18:45.519
You hear that?

983
01:18:45.521 --> 01:18:47.855
It's quiet in here.

984
01:18:47.857 --> 01:18:51.492
Boris! Boris!

985
01:18:51.494 --> 01:18:53.527
How did you feel?

986
01:19:59.961 --> 01:20:02.997
How you doing, Bobby?

987
01:20:02.999 --> 01:20:05.599
- I'm not going to do it anymore.
- Do what?

988
01:20:05.601 --> 01:20:07.735
Play with all these distractions.
It's like a circus.

989
01:20:08.937 --> 01:20:11.605
It's not what we agreed to.

990
01:20:11.607 --> 01:20:14.108
You know, they've been
doing this to me for years.

991
01:20:14.110 --> 01:20:16.243
Making me all these promises.
They all turn out to be lies.

992
01:20:16.245 --> 01:20:18.646
They've been doing it to me
since I was a kid.

993
01:20:18.648 --> 01:20:21.849
You know, I would get stuck in
Europe with huge hotel room bills

994
01:20:21.851 --> 01:20:24.451
when I was 15 years old.

995
01:20:24.453 --> 01:20:29.523
The federation would raise money off
of my back and funnel it to Reshevsky.

996
01:20:29.525 --> 01:20:31.826
Another Jew, by the way. I
don't know what I did to them.

997
01:20:31.828 --> 01:20:34.962
And they're all out to screw me.
The Russians and the Jews...

998
01:20:34.964 --> 01:20:37.364
they got their dirty little
fingerprints all over everything.

999
01:20:38.099 --> 01:20:39.767
What are we talking about?
The Jews.

1000
01:20:39.769 --> 01:20:41.635
Ah.

1001
01:20:41.637 --> 01:20:43.838
You made a stupid mistake, and
you got your ass handed to you.

1002
01:20:43.840 --> 01:20:45.840
No. I am not going to submit
to their game.

1003
01:20:45.842 --> 01:20:48.275
- If you don't get your head in the game...
- We're going to play my way!

1004
01:20:48.277 --> 01:20:49.844
You're not just gonna lose!
No audience, no distractions!

1005
01:20:49.846 --> 01:20:52.146
Humiliated in front
of billions of people!

1006
01:20:52.148 --> 01:20:56.450
You guys brought me here under false
pretenses, and you're going to fix it!

1007
01:20:57.719 --> 01:20:59.887
This is how I play!

1008
01:21:07.530 --> 01:21:11.398
You know, his mistake is that
he didn't see this bishop.

1009
01:21:13.535 --> 01:21:16.570
Unless the rest of the games are played
without an audience and without cameras,

1010
01:21:16.572 --> 01:21:17.972
he isn't going to show up.

1011
01:21:21.509 --> 01:21:23.911
He won't continue unless the games
are played in the ping-pong room?

1012
01:21:23.913 --> 01:21:26.013
That's the only place
that's quiet, he says.

1013
01:21:26.015 --> 01:21:28.015
He also wants
a different board.

1014
01:21:28.017 --> 01:21:32.086
Makes too much noise when he puts
the pieces down. He prefers wood.

1015
01:21:33.355 --> 01:21:36.190
It's like morphy.
It's destroying his brain.

1016
01:21:36.192 --> 01:21:38.559
No, chess isn't destroying him.
Why all the demands?

1017
01:21:38.561 --> 01:21:41.028
It's like he wants them
to say no.

1018
01:21:41.030 --> 01:21:43.697
I think he's afraid of
what's gonna happen if he loses.

1019
01:21:43.699 --> 01:21:45.799
No. He's afraid of what's
going to happen if he wins.

1020
01:21:58.079 --> 01:22:01.782
If the press could
please take their seats.

1021
01:22:01.784 --> 01:22:05.386
The match was scheduled
to begin at 5:00.

1022
01:22:05.388 --> 01:22:07.588
We will now start the clock.

1023
01:22:35.784 --> 01:22:40.254
Mr. Fischer failed to show.

1024
01:22:40.256 --> 01:22:43.057
Mr. Spassky wins by forfeit.

1025
01:22:43.059 --> 01:22:48.462
According to federation rules,
Mr. Fischer has 24 hours

1026
01:22:48.464 --> 01:22:51.465
to indicate in writing
that he wishes to continue,

1027
01:22:51.467 --> 01:22:54.501
otherwise Mr. Spassky
will be declared world champion.

1028
01:22:56.738 --> 01:22:59.239
<i>The match between Boris Spassky
and Bobby Fischer</i>

1029
01:22:59.241 --> 01:23:02.643
<i>has taken
a decidedly disappointing turn.</i>

1030
01:23:02.645 --> 01:23:04.445
<i>News in Reykjavik, Iceland
today,</i>

1031
01:23:04.447 --> 01:23:06.480
<i>Bobby Fischer failed to show up.</i>

1032
01:23:06.482 --> 01:23:08.849
<i>The match is Spassky's,
regardless of what happens now.</i>

1033
01:23:08.851 --> 01:23:11.418
<i>A two-point advantage is considered
practically insurmountable.</i>

1034
01:23:16.125 --> 01:23:19.093
<i>Unpredictable Bobby Fischer
stayed in bed today</i>

1035
01:23:19.095 --> 01:23:20.861
<i>rather than show up for game 2</i>

1036
01:23:20.863 --> 01:23:23.864
<i>in his world title match with
chess champion Boris Spassky.</i>

1037
01:23:23.866 --> 01:23:26.700
<i>The future of the match
now is clouded in doubt,</i>

1038
01:23:26.702 --> 01:23:29.036
<i>although game 3 is still
scheduled for Sunday.</i>

1039
01:24:14.150 --> 01:24:16.350
Bobby?

1040
01:24:16.352 --> 01:24:20.320
I have messages from people all over the
world who want you to open this door.

1041
01:24:24.326 --> 01:24:25.926
Bobby?

1042
01:24:49.584 --> 01:24:53.520
The president of the United
States called three times.

1043
01:24:53.522 --> 01:24:56.223
Three.

1044
01:24:56.225 --> 01:24:58.959
Uh, well,
he's been trying to reach you.

1045
01:25:01.129 --> 01:25:09.129
In Moscow, Brezhnev opened his only
bottle of 1868 Louis Roederer,

1046
01:25:10.139 --> 01:25:12.072
left over from the revolution.

1047
01:25:12.074 --> 01:25:16.710
You know why?
Because he heard you quit.

1048
01:25:19.447 --> 01:25:22.883
There are boys your age in Vietnam
giving their lives right now

1049
01:25:22.885 --> 01:25:24.551
in the fight against communism,

1050
01:25:24.553 --> 01:25:27.387
and all you have to do
is play a game of chess.

1051
01:25:38.299 --> 01:25:42.035
Bobby, I've been arguing
on your behalf all night long!

1052
01:25:45.140 --> 01:25:47.674
I-I've been
threatening them for you.

1053
01:25:47.676 --> 01:25:51.979
I have cajoled. I have begged them!
Now I'm begging you.

1054
01:25:51.981 --> 01:25:56.617
Please, please, please.

1055
01:25:56.619 --> 01:25:58.485
Go back in there and play.

1056
01:26:01.823 --> 01:26:03.524
You're one of them, aren't you?

1057
01:26:03.526 --> 01:26:05.726
- What?
- Who got to you?

1058
01:26:07.195 --> 01:26:10.664
- Kgb? CIA?
- Are you serious?

1059
01:26:16.504 --> 01:26:19.106
<i>This is</i> BBC news.

1060
01:26:19.108 --> 01:26:22.643
<i>The world chess championship looks
to be ending in humiliation</i>

1061
01:26:22.645 --> 01:26:25.145
<i>for the American Bobby Fischer</i>

1062
01:26:25.147 --> 01:26:29.283
<i>whose demands that the game be held in a
rec room have led to widespread ridicule.</i>

1063
01:27:28.209 --> 01:27:31.211
Huh?

1064
01:27:44.125 --> 01:27:45.993
Ah.

1065
01:29:33.868 --> 01:29:36.303
Stop here.

1066
01:29:36.305 --> 01:29:39.906
Thank you. Oh, hey.

1067
01:29:39.908 --> 01:29:42.976
You won't believe it. Spassky's
agreed to play in the ping-pong room.

1068
01:29:45.012 --> 01:29:46.213
Yeah. Right.

1069
01:29:46.215 --> 01:29:48.782
Wait. Where are you going?

1070
01:29:50.818 --> 01:29:53.019
Only one camera.

1071
01:29:53.021 --> 01:29:55.255
It does not move.
It makes no sound.

1072
01:29:59.594 --> 01:30:01.328
Good?

1073
01:30:03.097 --> 01:30:04.598
Good day. Thank you.

1074
01:30:08.870 --> 01:30:11.671
So please, gentlemen.

1075
01:30:11.673 --> 01:30:14.808
If we can remember,
the world is watching.

1076
01:30:22.683 --> 01:30:25.519
Did Nixon really call you?
Game's starting.

1077
01:31:15.136 --> 01:31:18.638
What's he doing? I don't know.

1078
01:31:28.149 --> 01:31:30.618
He's playing the Benoni.
Black, two games down.

1079
01:31:30.644 --> 01:31:31.852
He thinks it's suicide.

1080
01:31:31.854 --> 01:31:33.854
Is it suicide? Yeah.

1081
01:32:28.142 --> 01:32:30.176
- What's happening?
- He's exposing his king.

1082
01:32:30.178 --> 01:32:33.498
He's going to end up with doubled-up pawns.
He wants him to take it with the bishop.

1083
01:33:13.754 --> 01:33:16.022
Uh-huh. Yeah, shit.

1084
01:33:54.996 --> 01:33:57.631
Oh, my god.

1085
01:34:04.472 --> 01:34:08.308
He's threatening mate in one. He's going
to force the exchange of these knights.

1086
01:34:11.812 --> 01:34:13.580
He hates draws.

1087
01:34:16.117 --> 01:34:18.485
He takes him.

1088
01:34:28.195 --> 01:34:29.195
Check.

1089
01:35:10.538 --> 01:35:12.706
Yeah!

1090
01:35:19.748 --> 01:35:24.384
<i>Bobby Fischer beat Boris Spassky today in the
third game of the world chess championship.</i>

1091
01:35:24.386 --> 01:35:26.352
<i>Bobby Fischer won his first
game ever from Russia's...</i>

1092
01:35:26.354 --> 01:35:30.490
<i>Boris Spassky resigned today's
game on the 42nd move.</i>

1093
01:35:32.027 --> 01:35:34.561
<i>Fischer mania is taking
the country by storm</i>

1094
01:35:34.563 --> 01:35:37.731
<i>as news of Bobby Fischer's first
victory over Boris Spassky</i>

1095
01:35:37.733 --> 01:35:39.999
<i>has captured
the public's imagination.</i>

1096
01:35:42.036 --> 01:35:43.737
Bobby, I love you so much.

1097
01:35:43.739 --> 01:35:46.840
I know you're going to do amazing.
And win it for usa!

1098
01:35:46.842 --> 01:35:49.242
- Go usa!
- Number one!

1099
01:35:49.244 --> 01:35:51.010
Bobby! Bobby!

1100
01:35:51.012 --> 01:35:54.247
<i>A chess craze
is sweeping the nation.</i>

1101
01:35:54.249 --> 01:35:58.952
<i>You can find it being played in every
living room, park and classroom.</i>

1102
01:35:58.954 --> 01:36:02.388
<i>And the young man from Brooklyn,
little known until about a month ago,</i>

1103
01:36:02.390 --> 01:36:05.925
<i>is quickly becoming the most
famous celebrity in the world.</i>

1104
01:36:09.631 --> 01:36:12.999
<i>You can't imagine the tension involved
in a head-to-head match like this.</i>

1105
01:36:15.469 --> 01:36:18.438
Excuse me? Spassky
took Bobby's bishop.

1106
01:36:18.440 --> 01:36:20.607
Yeah, well,
I took his virginity.

1107
01:36:20.609 --> 01:36:23.309
Oh, wait.
Bobby's making a move.

1108
01:36:23.311 --> 01:36:25.812
Excuse me. They drew game
4 last night, you know?

1109
01:36:25.814 --> 01:36:27.981
Yeah, we know, asshole.
We're watching the rerun.

1110
01:36:57.077 --> 01:36:58.645
Hmm?

1111
01:38:13.354 --> 01:38:14.787
<i>Schieكe.</i>

1112
01:38:17.391 --> 01:38:20.660
Game 5 goes
to Mr. Bobby Fischer.

1113
01:38:45.719 --> 01:38:48.187
Mr. Spassky.

1114
01:38:48.189 --> 01:38:53.092
An electrical engineer examined the lighting
fixture and made an astounding discovery.

1115
01:38:53.094 --> 01:38:56.029
Inside, he found this.

1116
01:38:56.031 --> 01:38:57.597
Two dead flies.

1117
01:39:04.271 --> 01:39:06.572
<i>Bobby Fischer's
comeback in this match.</i>

1118
01:39:06.574 --> 01:39:09.042
<i>That's historic in chess.
Certainly, Jim.</i>

1119
01:39:09.044 --> 01:39:12.045
<i>And at this stage it looks as
though whoever wins this game...</i>

1120
01:39:12.047 --> 01:39:14.480
<i>Will most likely go on to
take the entire match.</i>

1121
01:39:14.482 --> 01:39:16.975
<i>This one game, game 6, will
determine the outcome?</i>

1122
01:39:17.001 --> 01:39:18.051
<i>I certainly think so.</i>

1123
01:39:18.053 --> 01:39:20.653
<i>Well, now we'll just
have to see what happens.</i>

1124
01:39:20.655 --> 01:39:22.989
<i>Fischer has agreed to return
to play in the main hall.</i>

1125
01:39:25.225 --> 01:39:27.627
Press could step back, please?
Thank you.

1126
01:40:23.050 --> 01:40:24.817
What's he doing?

1127
01:40:24.819 --> 01:40:27.353
<i>We all can't quite
believe it up here, Jim,</i>

1128
01:40:27.355 --> 01:40:29.889
<i>but in this,
the pivotal game of the match,</i>

1129
01:40:29.891 --> 01:40:33.393
<i>Fischer has abandoned
his trademark sicilian opening.</i>

1130
01:40:33.395 --> 01:40:37.764
<i>All of Spassky's careful preparation
is suddenly out the window.</i>

1131
01:40:58.318 --> 01:41:00.920
<i>Grandmasters are shaking
their heads in confusion.</i>

1132
01:41:00.922 --> 01:41:03.589
<i>Fischer's moves are
unprecedented in chess history.</i>

1133
01:41:03.591 --> 01:41:07.226
<i>No one seems to be able
to decipher what he's doing.</i>

1134
01:42:54.034 --> 01:42:55.268
Bravo.

1135
01:43:11.218 --> 01:43:14.120
<i>Grandmasters never applaud
an opponent's victory,</i>

1136
01:43:14.122 --> 01:43:18.057
<i>but I guess this is something
no one has ever seen before.</i>

1137
01:44:25.759 --> 01:44:28.527
- Bobby?
- Can I get you anything?

1138
01:44:28.529 --> 01:44:31.931
Oh, no, thanks.

1139
01:44:31.933 --> 01:44:35.468
It'll be light soon. Yeah,
it's never really dark here.

1140
01:44:51.051 --> 01:44:52.952
You know, Bobby,

1141
01:44:55.722 --> 01:44:57.990
the chess you've been playing
is really inspired.

1142
01:45:08.568 --> 01:45:12.138
It's almost all, um,
theory and memorization.

1143
01:45:13.674 --> 01:45:16.309
People think there are
all these options, but...

1144
01:45:18.945 --> 01:45:20.880
There's usually one right move.

1145
01:45:22.349 --> 01:45:24.550
Of course, in the end,
there's no place to go.

1146
01:47:31.878 --> 01:47:35.214
<i>The United States is based
on lies. It's based on theft.</i>

1147
01:47:35.216 --> 01:47:38.884
<i>Look at all I've done for the us. But
now I'm not useful anymore, you see?</i>

1148
01:47:38.886 --> 01:47:42.188
<i>The cold war is over, and now
they want to put me in prison.</i>

1149
01:47:44.825 --> 01:47:48.494
<i>I'm not sure, but if I know
the Jews in the us,</i>

1150
01:47:48.496 --> 01:47:52.298
<i>they may come at me
with some attack I don't expect.</i>

1151
01:47:52.300 --> 01:47:55.935
<i>Life is like that, and people
are living in a dream world.</i>

1152
01:47:55.937 --> 01:47:58.504
It's not really paradoxical.

1153
01:47:58.506 --> 01:48:02.007
Chess is, uh, basically
a search for truth, right?

1154
01:48:02.009 --> 01:48:05.010
- Right.
- So I'm searching for the truth.
